# HQL extensions and namespace initialization

Extensions provide capabilities outside the small [[hql/core]]. They may be
implemented in Rust, but HQL users should interact with ordinary typed functions
and values. Rust traits, lifetimes and implementation details do not become HQL
language requirements.

## The registry

A registry of extensions is implemented; a module loader is not. An extension
declares a name, a version and its steps, and each step supplies a structural
`StepSpec`, its evaluator, and whether it is pure — so a
capability is added without editing the core's checker or evaluator. Extensions
are compiled into the binary and registered in one table. There is no dynamic
loading and no plugin ABI: registration says what exists, and it is a host
mechanism that never reaches HQL's surface.

The signature declares generic parameters and bounds, input and result types,
argument names and arity, and lambda or type arguments. The checker and
`hql builtins` consume the same declaration. Ordinary steps need no custom
checker; dependent hooks add constraints such as intrinsic ordering or a
configured semantic index. Invalid signatures are rejected before checking a
program. Unknown, duplicate, and excess arguments are errors rather than ignored
syntax. Evaluators receive the checked execution tree, with resolved step and
type identities.

Registration is not import. Every extension in the binary is registered;
a program resolves what it has imported. That is what lets a diagnostic name
the import a program is missing instead of guessing at a misspelling.

## How an HQL interface is wired to its implementation

**HQL declares, an extension implements.** The interface of a module is its
HQL file; the implementation is native, inside an extension, and is reached
only through the declaration ([HQL-0024](../../proposals/HQL-0024/README.md),
parts 1, 3 and 9). The wiring, as the binary does it:

| In the `.hql` contract | In the extension | Matched by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| the module, `std/string.hql` is `string` | the descriptor's `provides: &["string"]` | the canonical module name; one extension per module, any number of modules per extension |
| an external declaration, `fn trim(self) : String` | an `Operation` named `trim` | the name; every declaration must be implemented and nothing else may be |
| `self`, or the first parameter where there is no `self` | the operation's input, `P::Type(STRING)` | position: the receiver module's pattern is the input's type |
| the parameters after it, in order | the operation's arguments, in order; a named argument by its name | arity: the written count lies between the required and the declared arguments, or the last is variadic on both sides |
| the result type | the operation's output | the result's constructor, where the output names one; an output that keeps or maps the input is not compared |
| a declared type without fields, `type File : {Replayable, Writable}` | a `Value` representation and the constructor step that builds it, `file(path)` | the type's name; the extension may not add a type, a parent or a field |

Binding runs once per process, before the first program, and a disagreement
refuses every program with a message naming the module, the declaration and
the extension. It replaces no test: `tests/declarations.rs` still compares the
names, and the binding is what runs in production.

A worked example across the three kinds of declaration:

```hql
// std/string.hql: the module is a receiver module over String, and every
// function is external.
module for String
fn trim(self) : String
fn pad_left(self, width: Int, fill: String) : String
```

```rust
// src/extensions/string.rs: the extension named `string` provides the
// module `string`; `trim` and `pad_left` are its operations, the input is
// `self`, and the two arguments of `pad_left` follow in order.
static STRING: Extension = Extension { name: "string", provides: &["string"], steps: string::STEPS, .. };
Operation { name: "pad_left", spec: OperationSpec { input: P::Type(STRING), arguments: &[width, fill], output: O::Type(P::Type(STRING)), .. }, .. }
```

Calling `"x".pad_left(3, "0")`, `string.pad_left("x", 3, "0")` or
`"x" | pad_left(3, "0")` resolves the declaration and runs the operation.

```hql
// std/io/mod.hql: a type the extension represents, and the constructor that
// builds it, declared here and implemented by the `io` extension.
type File : {Replayable, Writable} { path : String }
// file(path: String) : File
```

The `io` extension holds the representation, `Value::Endpoint`, and the step
`file`; a program never sees either, only `File` and `file(p)`.

```hql
// std/core.hql: a trait the language owns.
trait Ord {
    fn compare(self, other: Self) : Ordering
}
```

`Ord` for `Int`, `Float` and `String` is implemented natively, through the
values' own order; `impl Ord for Version { … }` in a program is an HQL body
run by the runtime; both are reached by `compare`, `<`, `sort`, `min` and
`max` through the one declaration. What part 1 also allows, an `impl` header
declared in HQL with a body an extension supplies, is not yet built, and
neither are extensions outside the binary: today every extension is compiled
in and the descriptor is the `provides` field of its static table entry, the
first stage of part 9's plan.

Steps split by the layering core → graph → knowledge:

| Provider | Steps | How a program gets it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| core | `size` `contains` `get` `count` `sort` `take` `filter` `map` `of_type` `is` `group` `reduce` `sum` `min` `max` | always |
| `graph` | `uplinks` `downlinks` `expand` | the prelude |
| `format` | `format` `text` `markdown` `hmd` `json` `yaml` `csv` `d2` `mermaid` | the prelude |
| `io` | `file` `source` `tap` `target` `sink` | the prelude |
| `net` | `http` `websocket` `socket` | `import net` |
| `logging` | `Logger` `trace` `debug` `info` `warn` `error` | `import logging` |
| `lexical` | `lexical` | `import lexical` |
| `semantic` | `semantic` | `import semantic` |

Collection operations stay in the core because they operate on the core's own
collection types and mention nothing above them. Retrieval and drawing are not
the core's business — see [[lexical]], [[semantic]], [[hql/semantic-search]]
and [[hql/presentation]].

`import <module>` makes a module visible under its last segment or an `as`
alias, `import io.net as net`, and `module.f(…)` is always available for one
that is imported. A builtin module is the extension of that name; a workspace
module is an HQL file under a module root, `hql.toml` `[modules] roots` or
`--modules`. Two visible modules may declare one function name; the input's
type tells them apart at the call, and a call that fits both is refused
naming both ([HQL-0024](../../proposals/HQL-0024/README.md), parts 1 and 2). A vault states its own imports in
`hql.toml`, and may decline the prelude. The rules are in
[the grammar](../../models/behavior/expressions.md) and
[the command line](../../models/behavior/cli.md); the decision is
[HQL-0001](../../proposals/HQL-0001/README.md).

An extension declares each step `Pure` or `Effectful`, because [[pipes]]
reserves execution freedom that is only safe over pure steps. Every step
registered today is pure. [HQL-0004](../../proposals/HQL-0004/README.md) adds a
shape beside purity — elementwise, partition, reduction or barrier — and the
backend a checked program runs on. The map/reduce vocabulary and the backend
contract are one Rust module, `mapreduce`; its steps are the core's, and the
module is how the binary is organized, not something a program imports.

An extension may also contribute metadata while cards are assembled; lexical
owns its own model and indexing entries. Extension-defined types and dynamically
registered document sources are not implemented and need a decision of their own.

An HQL module organizes reusable HQL definitions. An extension connects HQL to
external capabilities: for example, a data source, a renderer or an explicit
persistence operation. These are candidate uses, not a bundled extension list.
An extension may expose pure operations as well as effects.

## Namespace initialization

Namespace initialization is unimplemented and independent of the registry
above: it needs module resolution, which the registry deliberately does not
provide. A vault or namespace may contain `init.hql`. Its intended role is to
import the modules and extensions available to that namespace. This gives the namespace
an explicit entry point for its evaluation environment.

Illustrative layout:

```text
vault/
  init.hql
  alice.hmd
  research/
    init.hql
    notes.hmd
```

Illustrative contents of `init.hql`, using candidate module-import syntax:

```hql
import people
import graph
```

The names represent available modules or extension interfaces; their discovery
and installation are separate host responsibilities. Import syntax, exports,
qualification and any distinction between module and extension imports remain
open. An import is not implicitly a package download.

The research namespace can have its own imports. Whether it inherits the vault's
imports, how names shadow or conflict, initialization order, cycles, reloads and
failure handling all require decisions. The presence of nested `init.hql` files
alone does not settle those policies. Whether initialization permits work beyond
imports is also unresolved.

## Environments and capabilities

Initialization establishes namespace availability. The bridge into card and cell
environments still needs a precise contract. Cells close over their defining card
environment, including explicit imports and bindings. Locals can shadow names;
transclusion preserves the defining closure. Namespace initialization must remain
compatible with those lexical rules. Card filenames never implicitly bind names.

The extension boundary should expose typed inputs, outputs and failures. It also
needs a way to distinguish pure computation from effects so [[pipes]] can be
optimized safely. Importing a capability and invoking an effect are separate
operations. Reading, network access, printing and saving need explicit host
contracts; importing a renderer does not itself render a value.

The host adapter supplies typed HMD documents using the existing parser/resolver.
An extension should operate through that boundary rather than make raw text
parsing part of the core. Native ABI versus another transport, registration,
versioning, resource limits and capability policy remain open. This document does
not choose a Rust plugin ABI or prescribe a trait hierarchy.

See [architecture](../../models/domain/architecture.md),
[cell environments](../../models/behavior/program-values.md) and
[[hql/design-status]].
